    "https://www.schooljobs.com/careers/stjohns/jobs/5459982/executive-director-of-university-events": {
      "title": "Executive Director of University Events",
      "description": "Job Opportunities |       . For full functionality of this page it is necessary to enable JavaScript. \n        Here are the instructions how to enable JavaScript in your web browser Executive Director of University Events |       . page has loaded. Executive Director of University Events |       . page has loaded. Click here or press any key to continue. Job Opportunities Menu Job Opportunities Sign In Don't have an account? Create one . Already have an account? Sign In . User profile dropdown menu for Username Applications Inbox Profile Account Sign Out After clicking on this link you will be logged out and the page will be reloaded. Applications Inbox Profile Go to Background Investigation Account Sign Out After clicking on this link you will be logged out and the page will be reloaded. Welcome! Thanks for creating an account. . powered by NEOGOV ® Need this page in another language? You can translate this page using your browser's built-in translation feature. Tip: In Chrome or Edge, right-click anywhere on the page and select \"Translate to [Language].\" Executive Director of University Events Print Apply ﻿ Executive Director of University Events Salary $127,000.00 - $140,000.00 Annually Location Queens Campus, NY Job Type Full-Time Administrator Job Number 202600040 Org Office of University Events Opening Date 08/25/2026 Description Benefits St. John’s, established in 1870, has two New York City campuses; international locations in Rome, Italy; Limerick, Ireland; and Paris, France; and study abroad locations around the world. The Princeton Review and other top rankings consistently recognize the University’s outstanding academics, diverse student body, dynamic internship, and volunteer opportunities, focus on student life, and diverse study abroad offerings. St. John’s University offers more than 100 undergraduate and graduate programs in its six colleges and schools, with a growing number of programs offered online. The University is accredited by the Middle States Commission on Higher Education and 12 other major academic and professional associations. A dedication to diversity, equity and inclusion is at the heart of our mission. As a Catholic and Vincentian university, St. John’s is committed to institutionalizing practices of inclusive excellence to ensure that we welcome and celebrate the intrinsic worth of all members of our community. We will become an even stronger university as we enhance equity at every level of our institution.Our graduates will excel in the competencies and values needed for leadership and service in a rapidly evolving world. Job Summary The Executive Director serves as the University’s senior event leader, providing strategic direction, institutional governance, and executive oversight for all presidential-level and other high-impact University events, including commencement ceremonies and other major institutional events. Reporting to and closely aligned with the Vice President, Chief of Staff, and University Secretary, in the Office of the President, this role is responsible for setting vision, priorities, and standards for the University’s event portfolio while ensuring consistent, high-quality execution and protection of the University’s reputation. This position provides strong executive leadership over the event team, with a clear expectation of effective delegation, performance management, and accountability across all assigned staff and event portfolios. The Executive Director is ultimately accountable for outcomes, but achieves results by building capacity within the team, empowering leaders at all levels, and ensuring decisions are made at the appropriate level, rather than through personal control of day-to-day tactics. As the strategic lead for university events, the Executive Director operates at the portfolio and enterprise level, translating presidential and University priorities into cohesive event strategies, setting standards and protocols, managing risk, and guiding teams to deliver results within defined frameworks. This role requires the ability to move fluidly between strategy and execution, stepping in directly where necessary for the most sensitive or high-stakes events, while maintaining focus on long-term planning, organizational effectiveness, and continuous improvement. The Executive Director leads cross-functional collaboration with academic and administrative partners, exercises sound financial oversight of event budgets and resources, and serves as a trusted advisor to senior leadership. A core responsibility of the role is ensuring that institutional protocol, quality control, and risk assessment are embedded into all planning and execution, and that leaders are equipped with clear, timely, and decision-ready information. This position reflects a deliberate shift toward a tiered, standards-driven, governance-oriented, and data-informed model for managing University events. In its early phases, the Executive Director is responsible for building and operationalizing the governance framework, establishing clear roles, decision rights, and performance expectations, and strengthening the Office’s capacity to operate effectively without over-reliance on any single individual. The role is expected to lead at a strategic and enterprise level, setting direction, priorities, and standards; developing leaders within the team; and ensuring accountability for results. Execution is intentionally focused on institutional-level and high-stakes events, with the Executive Director engaging directly where executive judgment, visibility, or risk requires it, while otherwise ensuring work is appropriately delegated and managed. Central to this work is improving efficiency, enforcing portfolio discipline, and implementing meaningful measures of success to guide future investment, resource allocation, and decision-making. While ultimately accountable for outcomes, the Executive Director is expected to delegate execution responsibility to portfolio-owning leaders, intervening directly only when institutional risk, protocol exception, or executive visibility requires. Job Responsibilities Leadership • Serve as the primary point of coordination between the Office and the Office of the President. • Collaborate with the Vice President, Chief of Staff, and University Secretary to translate University and presidential priorities into a cohesive, intentional event portfolio. • Build a high-functioning leadership team and operational structure that enables the Office to perform effectively without over-reliance on the Executive Director for day-to-day tactical decision-making. • Provide direct leadership and oversight of all professionals within the Office. • Ensure teams are on track with planning, execution, timelines, and deliverables across all assigned event portfolios. • Set and enforce event priorities, sequencing, and scope decisions to balance institutional risk, capacity, and strategic value. • Coach, guide, and hold staff accountable for performance, quality, and adherence to institutional standards. • Reassign resources, step into execution roles, or provide hands-on support as needed to ensure successful outcomes. • Set annual priorities, goals, and operating plans aligned with the University’s Strategic Plan. Event Oversight & Execution • Maintain ultimate responsibility for the successful execution of all events owned by the Office. • Oversee event strategy and approve run-of-show development, scripting, guest experience, and institutional messaging for events. • Personally plan and execute select institutional and presidential-level events where executive oversight, sensitivity, visibility, or risk require direct involvement. • Ensure events are executed professionally, on time, within scope, and in alignment with University priorities and  expectations. • Ensure the President’s Office receives timely, accurate, and decision-ready event information, including calendars, briefs, scripts, guest lists, protocol considerations, risk factors, and supporting materials. • Anticipate needs and proactively manage executive readiness for major events. Event Governance, Standards & Protocol • Ensure the Office is established as a Center of Excellence, and lead the implementation and ongoing operation of the University’s event governance model, including standards, protocols, and decision-making frameworks. • Partner closely with the Manager of Protocol to ensure institutional protocol, academic tradition, and ceremonial standards  are consistently applied. • Serve as the final authority for protocol, standards, and exception decisions related to events. • Ensure that unit-led events subject to governance requirements comply with established institutional standards and expectations. • Identify and manage reputational, operational, and protocol-related risks associated with events, escalating issues as  appropriate. • Oversee the documentation and preservation of key institutional records related to major events, honors, awards, and ceremonies. Measurement & Assessment • Ensure that all Office-owned events have clear objectives, success measures, and post-event assessments, and that results are actively used to inform decision-making and performance accountability. • Lead post-event evaluations to assess effectiveness, outcomes, and opportunities for improvement, and use a Stop/Start/Continue framework to determine which events should be expanded, refined, consolidated, or discontinued based on institutional value and performance. • Use outcomes and ROI metrics to guide strategic decisions, resource allocation, and long-term portfolio strategy. Operations • Drive operational efficiency by improving processes, staffing models, technology use, cross-training, and scalable resourcing  approaches that reduce bottlenecks and support effective delegation. • Provide executive oversight of key vendors and external partners supporting events to ensure quality, performance, and alignment with institutional standards. • Oversee budgets, financial reporting, and resources associated with events. • Maintain a University-wide institutional event calendar and coordinate cross-unit dependencies. • Lead annual and multi-year planning for events, with attention to academic cycles, holidays, and institutional priorities. Committees • Serve as a member/chair of the institutional committees, including: o Commencement Committees o Honorary Degree and Commencement Speaker Committee o University Speaker Committee o President’s Society Selection Committee Perform other related duties as required in support of presidential and institutional priorities. Competencies, Education, Experience • Operates with executive presence, sound judgment, and discretion; serves as a trusted leader capable of setting priorities, making trade-offs, and driving strategy aligned with presidential and university goals. • Demonstrates a strong governance mindset with the authority and confidence to establish, enforce, and uphold institutional standards, protocols, and decision rights across a complex organization. • Understanding of academic tradition, institutional protocol, and reputational risk; serves as steward of ceremonial standards and quality assurance for high-visibility initiatives. • Exceptional diplomatic and interpersonal skills; able to lead through influence, navigate competing priorities, and manage senior-level stakeholders with clarity, trust, and credibility. • Proven ability to lead and coordinate complex, high-profile events with multiple internal and external stakeholders while maintaining accountability, timelines, and institutional alignment. • Experience defining KPIs, conducting post-event assessments, and using data to inform strategic planning, continuous improvement, portfolio decisions, and long-term resource allocation. • Demonstrated ability to evaluate financial, strategic, and operational return on investment to guide prioritization, budgeting, and portfolio optimization across institutional events. • Proven people leader with experience building, developing, and holding accountable high-performing teams; fosters clarity of roles, collaboration, and professional growth. • Strong written and verbal communicator, particularly at the executive-level; communicates with transparency, consistency, and institutional awareness. • Technologically adept and operationally fluent; able to translate strategic vision into executable plans while embracing innovation, new tools, and best practices that enhance quality, efficiency, and impact. Required Education and Experience • Bachelor’s degree required. • Minimum of 10 years of progressive leadership experience in higher education or a comparably complex, mission-driven organization. • Demonstrated leadership experience, including work with executive leadership, presidents, or equivalent governing bodies. • Demonstrated success leading high-visibility institutional initiatives. • Proven ability to manage teams, oversee execution, and deliver results in high-stakes environments. • Demonstrated experience supervising and developing professional staff. • Strong judgment, discretion, and comfort working closely with senior and executive leadership and leading across organizational boundaries. Preferred Education and Experience • Master’s degree preferred. • Experience with institutional events, protocol, governance, commencement, or major ceremonies. • Experience developing or applying performance measures, KPIs, or structured post-event evaluations. Supervisory Responsibility Yes Additional Eligibility Qualifications N/A Supplemental Information Work Environment This job operates in a professional office environment. This role uses standard office equipment such as personal computers, printers, etc. Physical Demands While performing the duties of this job, the employee is required to stand, move about, and communicate. The employee must lift or move up to 20-40 lbs. and perform moderate physical activity for event preparations and setups. Environmental Conditions N/A Position Type/Expected Hours of Work This is a full-time position in the office. Days and hours of work are Monday through Friday, 8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m. in the office. This position requires long hours and weekend work for event preparation and on event days. Attendance at events, and evening and weekend work is required. Work hours may vary based on operational needs. Travel Local and tri-state travel is required for off-site events, site visits, and event-related errands and meetings. *Important Note- St. John’s is NOT an E-Verify Employer St. John’s offers a competitive compensation program which is commensurate with your qualifications, experience, and contingent upon the departmental budget. St. John’s University considers factors such as (but not limited to) scope and responsibilities of the position, candidate’s work experience, education/training, key skills, internal peer equity, as well as market and organizational considerations when extending an offer. We also offer an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce. Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, faculty and staff can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstores, dining facilities, campus recreation and sporting events Any offer of employment is subject to receipt by St. John’s University of satisfactory references, verification of employment and education. St. John’s University is an Equal Opportunity Employer. All qualified applicants will receive consideration without regard to race, color, religion, sex, sexual orientation, gender identity, national or ethnic origin, age, status as an individual with a disability, protected veteran status, or any other characteristic protected by law. St. John’s offers an extremely comprehensive benefits program to meet the diverse needs of our workforce.  Along with exceptional benefits such as medical, dental, life insurance, long term disability insurance, tuition remission, generous 403(b) employer contribution, employee assistance program, and liberal paid time off policies, employees can also enjoy St. John’s performing arts, libraries, bookstore, dining facilities, campus recreation, and sporting events. Employer St. John's University (NY) Address 8000 Utopia Parkway Queens, New York, 11439 Phone (718) 990-2000 Website https://www.stjohns.edu/ Apply Please verify your email address Verify Email Job Opportunities Account Privacy Policy Terms Of Use Accessibility Statement Privacy Settings Copyright © 2026 Do not sell my personal information × For California residents, and to the extent NEOGOV sells your personal information as the term “sell” is defined under the California Consumer Privacy Act, you have the right to opt-out of the sale of your personal information by us to third parties at any time. This setting only applies to registered account holders that have opted-in for an Access Membership. If you wish to opt-out click follow these steps: Log into your Job Seeker Profile Click your name in the upper right-hand corner Select Account Settings Select the Preferences section Click the Edit button next to Access Membership Switch Toggle to off Click Save If you do not have a registered account this setting does not apply, however, you can review your right on our privacy policy . If you require further assistance, please email privacy@neogov.com × Accessibility Statement At Governmentjobs.com, Inc. d/b/a NEOGOV, we recognize the importance of making our products\n                    and services accessible to all individuals, including those with disabilities, and are continually\n                    working to improve the experience for all users. Commitment to Accessibility We are committed to following best practices, including the Web Content Accessibility Guidelines\n                    (WCAG) 2.1, Level AA, to the extent feasible, however, we do not warrant compliance with these\n                    standards or any other accessibility standards. We are continually enhancing our platforms to\n                    ensure greater accessibility, and we actively seek out opportunities to improve accessibility features. Ongoing Enhancements Accessibility is an ongoing effort, and we are regularly updating our technology, services, and content\n                    to address accessibility barriers. Our team is dedicated to identifying areas for improvement and\n                    making enhancements that allow all users, regardless of their abilities, to fully engage with our\n                    services. Feedback and Assistance We value feedback from our users. If you encounter any accessibility barriers while using our services\n                    or have suggestions on how we can improve, please reach out to us at accessibility@neogov.com.\n                    We will make every effort to resolve any concerns and consider alternative options where possible. Thank you for choosing NEOGOV. We remain dedicated to improving accessibility and creating an\n                    inclusive experience for all. . Job Opportunities Menu Close Menu . Job Opportunities Email Job Alerts St. John's University Website Help & Support Translate Support Job Application Job Details Applying as: | Support Close Close Cancel Clear SIGN Save We've updated our Privacy Policy and Terms of Use. Please, read and accept changes. Effective date: August 19, 2025 NEOGOV PRIVACY POLICY Contents 1. Introduction; Scope of this Policy. 2. Where We Are a Service Provider. 3. Personal Data We Collect About You and Why. 4. Cookies and Similar Tracking Technologies. 5. Online Analytics and Advertising. 6. Information We Disclose. 7. International Users. 8. Your Data Rights and Choices."
